# <%- nameClassName %>

Amazon Bedrock AgentCore Harness project generated by `@aws/nx-plugin:agentcore-harness`.

Generation only writes files. The Harness exists in AWS after you deploy the generated CDK construct or Terraform module through your normal infrastructure workflow; nothing in this project deploys, validates, or invokes a live Harness on its own.

## Targets

- `nx run <%- fullyQualifiedName %>:invoke -- "Your prompt"` invokes the deployed Harness (`invoke.ts`).
- `nx run <%- fullyQualifiedName %>:build` type-checks the project without contacting AWS.

All prompt arguments are joined with single spaces and trimmed into one user message. An empty or whitespace-only prompt exits with usage guidance before any AWS call.

## Harness discovery

The Invocation Client resolves the Harness ARN in this order:

1. `HARNESS_ARN` (non-empty): used directly; Runtime Configuration is not read.

   ```bash
   HARNESS_ARN=<harness-arn> nx run <%- fullyQualifiedName %>:invoke -- "Hello"
   ```

2. `RUNTIME_CONFIG_APP_ID`: reads the `agentcore` document of that AWS AppConfig application and uses the `harnesses.<%- nameClassName %>` entry published by the deployed infrastructure.

   ```bash
   RUNTIME_CONFIG_APP_ID=<application-id> nx run <%- fullyQualifiedName %>:invoke -- "Hello"
   ```

3. Neither: the command fails with an error naming both options.

The AWS Region is derived from the Harness ARN in any partition; no Region is hard-coded. Credentials come from the standard AWS SDK credential provider chain; none are stored in this project.

## Sessions

- Without `HARNESS_SESSION_ID`, each command generates a fresh UUID as the `runtimeSessionId`.
- The session ID is printed to stderr so you can copy it and continue the same session:

  ```bash
  HARNESS_SESSION_ID=<session-id> HARNESS_ARN=<harness-arn> nx run <%- fullyQualifiedName %>:invoke -- "Continue"
  ```

- A supplied `HARNESS_SESSION_ID` must contain between 33 and 100 characters, inclusive. Out-of-range values are rejected before any AWS call; in-range values are passed through unchanged.

## Output and exit codes

- stdout carries only the streamed response text, in delta order, with a trailing newline on success.
- stderr carries diagnostics: the session ID, Harness metadata, and errors.
- The exit code is the success signal. The command exits non-zero for a missing ARN source, an invalid session ID length, a malformed or region-less ARN, a missing event stream, service error events, an incomplete stream that ends without `messageStop`, or SDK request failures. Text already streamed stays on stdout even when the invocation fails.

## Permissions

The calling principal needs exactly `bedrock-agentcore:InvokeHarness` and `bedrock-agentcore:InvokeAgentRuntime` on the Harness ARN. The generated CDK construct's `grantInvokeAccess` grants exactly these two actions.

## Customizing the Harness

Deployment defaults (model, system prompt, allowed tools, execution limits) live in the generated infrastructure:

- CDK: `packages/common/constructs/src/app/harnesses/<%- nameKebabCase %>/<%- nameKebabCase %>.ts`. Pass native `CfnHarness` props for alternate model providers, tools, memory, skills, environments, truncation, custom JWT authorization, and execution limits; explicit props override the generated defaults.
- Terraform: `packages/common/terraform/src/app/harnesses/<%- nameKebabCase %>/<%- nameKebabCase %>.tf`. Set the module variables or add provider-native arguments in the documented extension region.

The service also accepts per-invocation overrides through `InvokeHarness` request fields; deployment defaults apply when a field is not overridden. If your application forwards per-invocation overrides from untrusted callers, validate and authorize them at the application layer.

## File ownership

Generated files (`invoke.ts`, `invoke-harness.ts`, `tsconfig.json`, this README, and the CDK construct or Terraform module) are yours after generation. Rerunning the generator never overwrites them; it only adds missing files and merges project metadata.
